title The mark travelled and the value did not
prompt the standing baton -- take the next agent-doable lap, reds first
think The lap opened onto a lock: an orphaned `--hot` roster pass from the previous lap, pid 2668359, ppid 1, eight minutes in. The card says hold still while a pass runs, so I held -- and it ran fifty-six minutes, closed `tree_moved=yes` because the previous lap's own commit landed under it, and its reading was therefore unusable. My own open ran `--scoped` off the 08:09 full-green receipt: 196 guards, 193 green, two red.
think Reds first sorted the two. `standing_equipment` reads `roster_broken` on an untracked run-card row naming `self_matching_assert`, a guard absent from tree and roster alike -- a red no tree edit can clear, carried forward by two peer seats already. `stash_record` reads `unlanded=4`: four finished laps in this tree's stash box that no commit carries.
think Reading the four settled it. `stash@{0}` was PHEROMONE's own lap of `20260909.230104`, touching `glow/rune_core.rye` -- my lane exactly -- with a `status GREEN` naming six witnesses and a red it had found and repaired. Recovering lost work in my own lane outranks a class repair on a peer's instrument, so I took it.
think Sole writer proven before anything moved: `sh tools/f/fleet_call.sh --pattern fleet-loop` with no signal named answered 21 peer trees `refused_foreign` and one live loop here, my own.
think The pop applied clean, and then `git fetch xy` said upstream had moved THIRTEEN commits. Fast-forwardable, so: stash again by name, `merge --ff-only`, pop. Four conflicts, each resolved toward the shared side -- upstream's card, upstream's ledger, upstream's fold recital, and my duplicate `%675` shelf deleted, since a peer had folded that row under its own name while my lap sat in the box.
think The send then met the same wall a second time. `git fetch xy` read SEVEN more commits, the rebase took five conflicts, and every one was a page peers and I both write: the card's live front, the ledger headline, the fold recital, and both session rosters. Each resolved to the shared side with my own block re-inserted, since a shared byte never yields to an unshared one.
think Measurement beats memory, so nothing was read off the recovered log. The witness ran, all five downstream importers of `rune_core` ran, and the three ratchets were re-read on the rebased tree rather than trusted: memcpy 135, `missing_assert_bind` 66 at its lowered ceiling, width-check ok.
obs The row could not be booked at the number it carried, and then could not keep the one it derived. `%676` was bound upstream to `20260909.174000` and the spine had spent through `%680`, so the row moved to `%681` -- and the rebase at the send found a peer had published `%681` at `20260910.001157` in the meantime, so it moved once more to `%682`. Three numbers, one stamp, and each move cost one edit rather than a sweep, which is the whole return on the derived-spine law.
obs Then the pin refused it. `REDS.md` stood at 40,959 of 40,960 with all sixteen rows OPEN, so no row was lawful to fold. The recovered row reads CLOSED, which made it the only foldable row in the ledger -- so it landed straight onto its own shelf and never rested on the pin. That is the ceiling speaking, and the shelf header says so rather than leaving it to be inferred.
obs The red itself is the marked-value foundation read from its failing side. `payload_len` 6, `payload_value` 5, `has_payload` true, and only the bytes wrong -- every derived field was computed before the frame died, so the mark crossed the seam intact while the value it named did not.
obs Against myself: while holding still for the roster I measured the census's quoted-argument walk end to end -- the prior lap's named handoff -- and built a working `live_lines` probe. The fetch at the send found a peer had landed the same shape upstream twenty-five minutes earlier, `13 -> 12`. Forty minutes spent on a lap already done. The instrument that would have prevented it is the fetch, and I ran it at the send rather than at the door.
rota lap 4499, row 4 -- Earth, the row that breathes in. Its threshold page says the concrete fact outranks the plausible story every time, and this lap had one of each: a stash entry that WAS a finished lap, and a measured forty-minute probe of work already landed. `the-marked-value.md` then named the red exactly -- every value crossing a seam wears a mark, and here the mark was true while the value was dead.
loom witness=glow_rune_core_witness verdict=green downstream=5 all_green
loom ratchet=memcpy_app_sites=135 missing_assert_bind=66 ceiling=66 over=0
loom guard=standing_equipment scoped_open guards_run=196 green=193 red=2 gated=1 seconds=2155
loom guard=stash_record before=4 after=3 stashes=19
obs THE HOT CLOSE FOUND FOUR MORE REDS AND THREE WERE MINE. `log_file_claim` caught the recovered log naming the %675 shelf I had just deleted as a duplicate -- a claim about a file that shipped, so the two file lines were rewritten to name what actually ships and the log says it was adjusted. `log_has_a_row` and `session_roster_agree` were one fault twice: a closed day's row count is spelled in two rosters and derived in neither, so adding a row to 20260909's shelf staled both. Derived rather than typed, 98 to 101 -- then 103 to 104 after the rebase brought peers' rows in, which is the same fault firing twice inside one send. All three re-proven GREEN individually before the second hot pass.
obs THE FOURTH IS A PEER'S AND MINUTES OLD. `instrument_refusal` reads `swallowed_instrument_passes=1` on `tools/fixtures/t/tutorial_output_scan.sh:180`, a `diff | sed >> file || true` that discards the instrument's own failure. That file landed in the thirteen commits I rebased onto; naming it beats racing the hand that is probably still holding it.
loom probe=live_lines corpus=3322 ground_state=3321 nonground=1 candidates_13_to_12 superseded_upstream=yes
file glow/rune_core.rye the assert bind, six accessor and mutation contracts, both memcpy sites migrated, and the use-after-return repaired by returning FaceSpec by value
file tools/fixtures/o/opening_lines_scan.rish missing_assert_bind ceiling lowered 67 to 66, printed line and comparison together
file .claude/rules/tame-guidance.md the memcpy figure refreshed to 135 and named a FREE number, so a reader runs the scan
file .cursor/rules/tame-guidance.mdc the same repair in the twin
file construction/archive/REDS-the-frame-that-died-holding-a-name-rows-682.md the row landed here rather than on the pin, and the header records its three numbers
file construction/ITINERARY.md the PHEROMONE account, elder shelved whole, and the nib moved to this round's parent
scope one finished lap recovered from this tree's stash box, rebased across thirteen upstream commits, renumbered against the anointed spine, re-proven on metal rather than trusted, and landed whole
status GREEN on what this lap owns -- glow_rune_core_witness and five downstream glow witnesses, tame_style_check, opening_lines_witness, width-check, ascii_document, reds_ledger_monotone, reds_citation, reds_spine_derive, reds_pin_capacity, reds_status_consistency, unshared_citation all green on metal after the rebase; plus log_file_claim, log_has_a_row and session_roster_agree, each red on the hot close and each re-proven green. Scoped cold open 193/196. THREE REDS STAND AND NONE IS CLOSABLE HERE: stash_record at unlanded=3, standing_equipment on an untracked run-card row naming a guard the tree never had, and instrument_refusal on a peer's scan landed in the commits I rebased onto
recommend keep-going -- the box holds three more unlanded laps, and `standing_equipment` still reds on an untracked run-card row naming a guard the tree never had. The second is the class repair worth a lap: a run-card row whose guard is absent from BOTH the roster and the disk is stale pier memory rather than a broken roster, and today no tree edit can clear it, so it reds every pier forever.
